## Summary of what the code shows

I could only use `rtk` (a grep wrapper — note it doesn't recurse into directories, so I had to name files explicitly), so everything below is verified from source text in this tree; I flag clearly where I cannot verify something.

## The chain

**1. UCSI work handler → partner unregister**

`drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c`:

```
static void ucsi_handle_connector_change(struct work_struct *work)
	if (change & UCSI_CONSTAT_CONNECT_CHANGE) {
		if (UCSI_CONSTAT(con, CONNECTED)) {
			ucsi_register_partner(con);
		} else {
			ucsi_unregister_partner(con);
```

and

```
static void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)
	if (!con->partner)
		return;
	typec_set_mode(con->port, TYPEC_STATE_SAFE);
	ucsi_unregister_altmodes(con, UCSI_RECIPIENT_SOP);
	ucsi_unregister_cable(con);
	typec_unregister_partner(con->partner);
	con->partner = NULL;
```

This matches the reported stack (`kworker/6:0`, `events`, `ucsi_handle_connector_change` → `ucsi_unregister_partner` → `typec_unregister_partner`). UCSI itself is *not* the bug: it correctly guards on `con->partner`.

**2. The offending function: `typec_unregister_partner()` → `typec_partner_unlink_device()`**

`drivers/usb/typec/class.c`:

```
void typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)
	mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);
	if (port->usb2_dev) {
		typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev);
		port->usb2_dev = NULL;
	}
	if (port->usb3_dev) {
		typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb3_dev);
		port->usb3_dev = NULL;
	}
	mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);
```

```
static void typec_partner_unlink_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)
	sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));
	sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");     <-- the "typec" name in the warning
	if (partner->deattach)
		partner->deattach(partner, dev);
```

The `'typec'` string in the warning comes from exactly one place in the whole file — this `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec")` (the only creator is the matching `sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, "typec")` in `typec_partner_link_device()`).

**3. Why ENOENT / "no directory"**

`sysfs_remove_link()` (`fs/sysfs/symlink.c`) does `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(kobj->sd, name, ns)`, and `fs/kernfs/dir.c:1706`:

```
	if (!parent) {
		WARN(1, KERN_WARNING "kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\n", name);
		return -ENOENT;
	}
```

So `kobj->sd == NULL` for the *USB device* (`port->usb2_dev` / `usb3_dev`) whose kobject sysfs dir has already been torn down, **or** the `typec` symlink was never successfully created.

**4. Why `kobj->sd` can be NULL / link absent — two verified holes**

(a) *Link creation is not tracked.* `typec_partner_link_device()`:
```
	ret = sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, "typec");
	if (ret)
		return;                                  /* silent, no state recorded */
	ret = sysfs_create_link(&partner->dev.kobj, &dev->kobj, dev_name(dev));
	if (ret) {
		sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");  /* rolled back */
		return;
	}
```
`struct typec_port` (`drivers/usb/typec/class.h`) only stores `struct device *usb2_dev; struct device *usb3_dev;` — there is **no** "link established" flag. So `usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` stay set even when linking failed/was rolled back, and `typec_unregister_partner()` later unlinks unconditionally → ENOENT.

(b) *No reference on the cached USB device, and a teardown race.* `typec_partner_attach()` just caches the pointer:
```
	struct usb_device *udev = to_usb_device(dev);
	if (udev->speed < USB_SPEED_SUPER)
		port->usb2_dev = dev;
	else
		port->usb3_dev = dev;
```
with no `get_device()`. The clearing path is `typec_partner_deattach()` (`port->usb2_dev == dev` → NULL), reached from `typec_deattach()` (`include/linux/usb/typec.h`) which is called in `usb_disconnect()`:
```
	usb_disable_device(udev, 0);
	typec_deattach(port_dev->connector, &udev->dev);
	usb_remove_ep_devs(&udev->ep0);
	device_del(&udev->dev);
```
`typec_deattach()` is a no-op unless `port_dev->connector` is set (`drivers/usb/core/port.c`, set only in `connector_bind()`, cleared in `connector_unbind()`). So if the component link is unbound, or the USB device dies without going through `port_dev->connector`, `device_del()` frees `udev->dev.kobj.sd` while `port->usb2_dev` still points at it. The subsequent UCSI disconnect event → `typec_unregister_partner()` → `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec")` on a `sd == NULL` kobject → the exact WARN. `port->partner_link_lock` serialises typec-side callers but gives no protection against USB-core teardown.

At boot on this machine both conditions are easy to hit: the UCSI connector-change work is queued very early (`ucsi_partner_task(con, ucsi_check_connection, 1, 0)` at registration) and races with USB enumeration/disconnect of the Type-C-attached device.

## Is a fix present in this tree?

What *is* present (so those earlier fixes are already in 6.17):
- `mutex partner_link_lock` in `struct typec_port`, taken in `typec_register_partner()`, `typec_unregister_partner()`, `typec_partner_attach()`, `typec_partner_deattach()` — the earlier partner-registration race fix.
- `if (con->partner) return 0;` in `ucsi_register_partner()` and `if (!con->partner) return;` in `ucsi_unregister_partner()` — double-register/unregister guards.

What is **not** present (the actual missing fix for this warning):
- no per-device "link created" state to make `typec_partner_unlink_device()` idempotent,
- no `get_device()`/`put_device()` on `port->usb2_dev` / `port->usb3_dev`,
- no `device_is_registered()` / `dev->kobj.sd` check before `sysfs_remove_link()` (grep for `device_is_registered|kernfs|kobj.sd` in `class.c` returns nothing).

So: **the root cause is unfixed in this tree**, in `typec_partner_unlink_device()` / `typec_unregister_partner()` / `typec_partner_attach()` in `drivers/usb/typec/class.c`.

## On the upstream commit

I cannot honestly cite an upstream commit hash or series: this environment gives me only `rtk` (grep over the working tree) — no git history, no network, no `Documentation`/changelog with that information. Any hash I produced would be fabricated. What I can say with confidence is that the fix must land in `drivers/usb/typec/class.c` and take one of these shapes (all consistent with how upstream has fixed the sibling races here):

1. Track link state per USB device (e.g. a bitfield/flag set only after both `sysfs_create_link()` calls succeed) and skip `typec_partner_unlink_device()` otherwise.
2. Take a reference: `get_device()` in `typec_partner_attach()`, `put_device()` in `typec_partner_deattach()`/`typec_unregister_partner()`.
3. Guard the removal: `if (dev->kobj.sd)` / `device_is_registered(dev)` before `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec")`.

If you give me shell/git access (`git log --oneline -- drivers/usb/typec/class.c`, or `git log --grep="can not remove"`), I'll pin the exact commit and tell you whether it's in 6.17.0-1011-oem or needs backporting. The warning itself is benign (`WARN(1)` + `-ENOENT`, no leak or crash) — it's cosmetic noise, not a functional Type-C failure.
